diff --git a/docs/data-cache.md b/docs/data-cache.md
index 4bbb53476a0eab3ce7eebceea65dc13cc73f6f37..d1efdcd24e4995fd0752322916adb8826c82c988 100644
--- a/docs/data-cache.md
+++ b/docs/data-cache.md
@@ -6,6 +6,9 @@ nav_order: 16
 # Data Cache

 A cache is a saved copy of work the reader has already done, so it does not have to repeat it. When you open a book for the first time, CrossInk works out where every page break goes and saves the result on the SD card. The next time, the book opens much faster.
+Library cover view uses three columns and nine books per page, sharing Home’s cover component and sizing. A low-priority worker creates EPUB/XTC thumbnails without accessing the renderer or input loop, prioritizing the visible page and then continuing through other pages while idle. Only one copied job is retained; thumbnails live in the existing per-book SD caches. In-progress BMP files are never painted. Library filtering processes one source book between input checks after the first paint. Books without covers use a placeholder; confirmed missing EPUB covers are remembered in `library-cover-missing`. Text files use a placeholder. Library opens the saved index on startup. After its first paint, a paced metadata-only check compares book paths, sizes, and modification timestamps with the saved index, then offers Refresh if they differ. Transfers and on-device file changes also offer Refresh. The check reads no book contents; edits retaining both size and timestamp may not be detected. No rebuild occurs until Refresh is selected unless the index is missing or incompatible. No cache format change or cache deletion is needed.
+
+CrossInk caches data aggressively on the SD card to minimize RAM use. The ESP32-C3 has about 380 KB of usable RAM, so rebuilding every book structure in memory on every open would be too expensive.

 Everything is kept in a hidden folder called `.crosspoint` on the SD card. It holds the caches and also your personal data: settings, reading progress, bookmarks, clippings, reading stats, and saved Wi-Fi networks. CrossInk does not change the contents of your book files.

@@ -73,3 +76,55 @@ book once so CrossInk can record where the existing stats came from. Files copie
 before that step have no origin information; the first updated reader to use them
 will claim them. This change prevents future duplicates and does not automatically
 correct totals already duplicated on the sync server.
+Library replaces the Recent Books screen.
+
+The Library reconciles its index with the SD card on the first visit after a cold boot,
+after file changes in File Browser, and after leaving a download or file-transfer
+screen. Ordinary return visits and deep-sleep wakes reuse a successfully scanned
+index. Failed or memory-limited scans are retried on the next visit. The Library's
+refresh action always scans again; use it
+if files were changed externally while the firmware stayed running or the reader
+was asleep.
+Unchanged books reuse their cached metadata. Each EPUB's parsed metadata is also
+saved to `library.meta`/`library.metd` as soon as it is read, so a scan that is
+cancelled, fails, or loses power picks up where it stopped next time instead of
+parsing every book again. Deleting both files is safe; they are rebuilt as books
+are parsed.
+**Library > Settings > Use Book Metadata** selects embedded EPUB titles and
+authors; disabling it uses filenames. TXT, Markdown and XTC files use filename
+fallbacks. CLX1 version 2 adds a first-name author permutation; older Library
+indexes rebuild automatically when Library opens.
+
+**Date Added** uses the file creation time captured by the index. Its
+first-seen sequence breaks ties and orders books without a creation time among
+themselves; those books appear before dated books in ascending order.
+The creation time is the filesystem's best available estimate of when a file
+arrived on the card; some copy tools may preserve the original timestamp.
+**Recently Opened** shows only books in the
+saved reading history (up to 18 books), newest first. Reversing that sort shows
+the same books oldest first. Marking a book unfinished does not add it to
+reading history; opening it does.
+Search matches words in the title and author and retains the selected sort.
+The sort method and direction are saved when changed. Library displays EPUB
+books. The header checkbox includes finished books when checked. Expanded
+Title and Author sorts show alphabetic headings.
+
+On button devices, Left opens sorting and Right toggles finished books.
+On touch devices, use the header buttons and the list view’s sort controls.
+Hold a book row or Confirm for book actions.
+
+The Lyra Carousel snapshot cache advances to version 7 so cached home menus
+regenerate with the Library label and landmark icon. No manual EPUB cache reset is required.
+
+## Library view snapshots
+
+`library-filter.cache` and `library-recents.cache` store completed filter membership
+and counts. Keys include the query, sort/direction, file filters, metadata preference,
+completion filter, and index header or recent-history contents. They do not contain
+book text or images. Index rebuilds/file changes and completion changes invalidate
+both snapshots; ordinary reading-time/page-count saves do not. A cold or invalid
+snapshot falls back to the existing incremental filter and is replaced on completion.
+Rendered cover pixels use the same optional PSRAM cache as Home. The first completed
+visible cover batch repaints once; later idle preloading does not repaint the screen.
+Count and page share one footer; status changes do not resize the grid and
+trigger another thumbnail-size conversion. Missing images display the title.
diff --git a/docs/file-formats.md b/docs/file-formats.md
index 705302a47f6b01f1be9184ab20c2a4390a339670..8ce817fafdd0306eed5d9762805c9146fc60ef56 100644
--- a/docs/file-formats.md
+++ b/docs/file-formats.md
@@ -2339,3 +2339,65 @@ The defined-property mask uses bit 31 for CSS visibility. In the background byte
 Mixed-case selectors now use the same comparison when merging and matching
 rules. Revision 24 rebuilds the rules and chapter layouts on book reopen so
 cached pages pick up the corrected styling. The binary format is unchanged.
+
+## EPUB progress.bin version 2 (crASHink)
+
+The 22-byte version-2 record begins with bytes `50 52 47 02` (`PRG`, version 2),
+then little-endian u16 spine, page and page count; u32 visible text offset
+(`0xffffffff` means absent); and u64 UTC milliseconds of the position edit
+(0 means unknown). The timestamp is written atomically with the position using
+the existing temp/backup rotation. Legacy 4-, 6- and 10-byte records remain
+readable with an unknown timestamp. Returning to the same text, opening menus
+and repagination preserve the edit time; applying Readest progress preserves
+its source time. A new reading position gets UTC milliseconds from a valid system clock, falling back to the hardware
+RTC (one second resolution). Timezone display
+settings do not affect this value.
+
+Latest-wins sync refuses to overwrite either side when an existing position
+has an unknown time. For legacy device records, turn a page after updating to
+establish a timestamp. If the clock is unset, sync network time first and then
+move to the desired position. Equal timestamps leave both sides unchanged.
+Older firmware cannot read version 2; retain the firmware and progress backup
+before downgrading. TXT/XTC progress formats are unchanged.
+
+## Library view cache v2
+
+Disposable files `/.crosspoint/library-filter.cache` and
+`/.crosspoint/library-recents.cache` use a packed 30-byte little-endian header:
+`magic:u32=0x3156434c`, `version:u16=2`, `payloadBytes:u16`, `key:u64`,
+`sourceCount:u16`, `visibleCount:u16`, `processedCount:u16`, `checksum:u64`.
+The checksum combines FNV-1a of the payload with the key, source/visible/processed
+counts and payload size. Partial snapshots resume from processedCount; full ones
+restore without a filter pass.
+The full-library payload is the existing filter bitmap (at most 4096 bytes),
+with sparse ranks reconstructed in RAM. The recent payload is the fixed
+18-entry u16 history-row array; only visibleCount entries are used. Wrong keys,
+counts, versions, lengths, truncation or checksum mismatch cause a cache miss.
+Writes use a temporary file and rename; interruption safely falls back to refiltering.
+This does not change the EPUB, stats or Library index binary format.
+
+
+## Opened-book ordering (OPB1, version 1)
+
+Home cover browsing uses `/.crosspoint/opened.idx`, newest opened first, plus
+`opened.paths`. No book contents, titles, authors or cover pixels are duplicated
+in these files. The normal library cover cache remains the source of thumbnails.
+
+The little-endian packed index header is 20 bytes: magic u32 `0x3142504f`,
+version u16 (1), entry stride u16 (20), entry count u32, revision u64. Each
+20-byte entry stores complete-path FNV-1a hash u64, path payload offset u32 and
+last-opening order u64. Opening order is a persistent monotonic counter, so
+sorting does not depend on the device clock being set. The newest-first index
+supports direct seeks to a page; opening Home does not walk the whole history.
+
+The append-only path file stores u16 byte length followed by UTF-8 path bytes
+(1–511 bytes). Paths are hash-checked when read. Reopening reuses the existing
+path payload and moves its small index entry to the front. Metadata refreshes
+of the current book do not rewrite the ordering. Rename keeps its position;
+removal drops only that entry. No fixed history-book count is imposed.
+
+Updates stream the small index through `opened.tmp`, sync it, rotate the old
+index to `opened.bak`, then publish. The old snapshot can be recovered if a
+write/rename is interrupted. An unpublished path tail is harmless. Legacy recent
+entries seed one complete snapshot, preserving order; later history comes from
+actual opens. History already evicted by older firmware cannot be recovered.
